course: dictionary
lesson: 1001
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Bold is a word that changes its meaning depending on where you find it. The words around it are its context, and they tell you which meaning to pick. «The gull is bold and clever, and it snatches chips from children's hands.» Bold here means brave and not afraid, because the gull is daring enough to grab food from people.
explain: Look at the sentence «A headline is short, bold and grabs attention.» A headline is a news title, so bold cannot mean brave. It means strong and easy to see, like the thick letters that catch your eye. Ask yourself: which meaning fits the words around it?
explain: Context can also tell you when bold is not a praise word. In Ireland a parent might say, «You're very bold!» to a child who is misbehaving. The clue is the parent's tone and the telling-off, so bold means naughty, not brave.
fact: Bold is an adjective when it describes something, as in «a bold knight».
fact: «Bold» means brave and confident, not afraid to take risks.
fact: «Bold» can also mean strong and easy to see, like bright colours or thick lines.
fact: Bold type is thick, dark print used to make words stand out, such as headings.
fact: In Ireland, «bold» can mean naughty, especially for children, and it can also mean cheeky or rude.
exercise: «They made so bold as to knock at Badger's back door.» What does bold mean here? | Very quiet | Brave enough to dare | *Dared to, which is brave enough to try | Thick and dark
exercise: «A headline is short, bold and grabs attention.» What does bold mean here? | Naughty | Brave and fearless | Very old | *Strong and easy to see
exercise: An Irish parent says, «You're very bold!» to a child. What does bold mean? | *Badly behaved, naughty | Strong and easy to see | Thick, dark print | Very brave
test: In «Gulls are bold and clever», what does bold mean? | Quiet and gentle | *Brave and not afraid to take risks | Very old | Thick and dark
test: «The suffragettes used bolder actions.» What does bolder mean here? | Older | *More daring | Thicker | Quieter
exam: Which use of bold means thick, dark print? | Don't be bold | The plan was bold | *Make the heading bold so it stands out | The knight was bold
